WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.530
- Good evening ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:05.310
Welcome to the 14th
Annual Governor's Awards.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.290
Please take your seat.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:11.790
Welcome everybody, to the
Dolby's Theaters holding room.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:15.600
This is a multipurpose room
for overflow of talent.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.750
Have a seat, thank you, find your tables.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.570
Ladies and gentlemen, find your tables.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.760
We have to be outta here by 10:00 PM.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:24.870
There is a corporate event

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.359
for some personal injury
attorneys coming in.

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:28.192
(audience laughs)

00:00:28.192 --> 00:00:30.450
They will be using the same decorations.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.540
Please find your seats, everybody.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.310
Let's all sit down.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.420
If you don't sit down,

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.100
I'm gonna bring Terrence Howard up here

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.430
and he'll explain his new
geometry and it's not fun.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.460
Everyone sit down, let's do this.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.910
Hello, ladies and
gentlemen, it is an honor,

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.830
and a favor to be here.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.785
This is a strategic obligation.

00:00:53.785 --> 00:00:58.710
It is a pleasure to stand
here on this temporary stage.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.690
For those of you that don't recognize me

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.882
from the Tuesday night AA
meeting in the Palisades,

00:01:03.882 --> 00:01:04.920
(audience laughs)

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.110
my name is John Mulaney.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.488
If you don't know, thank you.

00:01:08.488 --> 00:01:12.120
(audience cheers)

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.020
That's very nice.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.900
If you don't know who I
am, that's totally fine.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.790
I'm like Louis Farrakhan.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.966
I mean a lot to a very
small group of people.

00:01:20.966 --> 00:01:22.170
(audience laughs)

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:25.710
You know, it's such an honor
for me to be hosting this.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.810
Growing up as a boy,

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.960
I would always watch the
non-televised Governor's Awards,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.900
even though they only
started 14 years ago.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.100
My family, we'd gather in
front of the turned off TV.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.533
It was the only time I
ever saw my dad smile.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:45.450
And I'd think, "Someday,
that's gonna be me."

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.570
Look at this room full of
people, this is a solid room.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.120
We have so many fantastic
people in this room.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.040
Everybody in this room,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.340
knows something about
somebody else in this room,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.168
that nobody else knows.

00:01:58.168 --> 00:01:59.001
(audience laughs)

00:01:59.001 --> 00:02:00.630
Isn't that fun?

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.580
And if you don't know
something about someone else

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.170
in this room that no one else knows,

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.760
you should leave the business,

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.776
'cause you have absolutely
no pull whatsoever.

00:02:08.776 --> 00:02:09.609
(audience laughs)

00:02:09.609 --> 00:02:12.000
Look at the amazing talent in this room.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.010
Mr. Bradley Cooper is here.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.447
Bradley Cooper.

00:02:15.447 --> 00:02:17.790
(audience cheers)

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:22.260
The incredible director
and star of "Maestro,"

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.137
or as it was originally
titled, "Bye, Felicia."

00:02:26.866 --> 00:02:30.750
Bradley worked for six years,

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.860
to do six minutes of accurate
conducting in the film.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.670
That is, may I say something,
dude, that is very cool.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.890
Just FYI, none of us know

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.840
what accurate conducting looks like.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.720
You could have literally done anything.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:46.860
People were like, "This is the scene

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.180
where he perfectly conducts."

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.260
I was like, "I'll take your word for it."

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.093
(audience laughs)

00:02:51.093 --> 00:02:53.310
How about the cinematic masterpiece,

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:56.063
that is "Oppenheimer,"
ladies and gentlemen.

00:02:56.063 --> 00:02:57.360
(audience cheers)

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.683
Oh my God.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.700
Like a lot of you, I spent
the first two and a half hours

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:03.757
of Oppenheimer thinking,

00:03:03.757 --> 00:03:07.170
"They only gave Rami Malek one line?

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:08.850
Why did he show up for one line?

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.010
Is the catering good or something?"

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.250
That "Oppenheimer" cast
is stacked with talent.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.980
Every part is played by a huge movie star.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.240
They're like, "Hey, we need an extra

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.800
to polish the atom bomb.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.470
Let's see if Jack Nicholson is available."

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:25.830
Daniel Day-Lewis plays a pair of goggles.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:27.990
I'm fully kidding, he doesn't.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:30.030
Ladies and gentlemen, you're looking at me

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.770
and you're thinking, "Oh, great.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.920
Another goddamn standup comedian

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.650
here to make fun of us
at our sacred rituals."

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.370
Well, I'll have you know,
I'm not such an outsider.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:45.120
I am quite sought after in the film world.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.000
I would like to share
with you a real email

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.210
that I got from my
agents about 30 days ago.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:55.350
This is an actual email that
they sent me on December 7th,

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:58.574
so you'll get an idea of
who you're dealing with.

00:03:58.574 --> 00:03:59.697
(audience laughs)

00:03:59.697 --> 00:04:00.530
(John clears throat)

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.740
"John, in anticipation of
our conversation attached,

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.280
please find the untitled Maggie
Gyllenhaal project written

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.290
and to be directed by Maggie Gyllenhaal."

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.407
That makes sense.

00:04:11.407 --> 00:04:13.770
"Emma Tillinger Koskoff, Osnat Handelsman,

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.570
and Talia Kleinhandler are attached

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:17.490
to produce for Warner Brothers.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:18.900
Christian Bale is negotiating

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:20.880
to star opposite Jessee Buckley."

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.467
I know who one of those people is.

00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:27.300
"There is no script
available at this time."

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.342
Okay, I'm listening.

00:04:29.342 --> 00:04:31.477
(audience laughs)

00:04:31.477 --> 00:04:36.270
"Pending, pending your
response to the material.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:40.200
Please use the attached
sides to put yourself

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.391
on tape for the role of Young Cop."

00:04:44.391 --> 00:04:46.350
(audience laughs)

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:47.841
Let's, thank you.

00:04:47.841 --> 00:04:49.020
(audience cheers)

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.463
They really sent this to me.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.150
WME sent this to me, let me be clear.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.900
I'm not being offered Young Cop.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:57.733
(audience laughs)

00:04:57.733 --> 00:05:00.690
They're giving me the chance
to throw my hat in the ring.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:04.080
And yes, I put myself on tape,

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.011
and, no, I didn't get it, but...

00:05:08.011 --> 00:05:10.020
(audience laughs)

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.690
Let's stop focusing on failure, all right?

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:13.523
Enough failure.

00:05:13.523 --> 00:05:15.630
Tonight, there are only winners.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:19.950
We are honoring four
individuals tonight whose love

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:24.090
and labor and creativity made
an indelible mark in film.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.160
And by film I mean movies.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.980
Tonight we honor a man that I think

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.710
is the funniest person to ever live.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:33.598
Mr. Mel Brooks.

00:05:33.598 --> 00:05:36.431
(audience cheers)

00:05:41.911 --> 00:05:42.744
Mel.

00:05:46.237 --> 00:05:50.257
"The Producers," "Blazing
Saddles," "Young Frankenstein,"

00:05:50.257 --> 00:05:53.010
"Silent Movie," "History of the World."

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:54.873
Mel Brooks, ladies and gentlemen.

00:05:55.787 --> 00:05:58.620
(audience cheers)

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:16.580
Mel, Mel Brooks, or his
antisemites call him, "Exhibit A."

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:17.760
(audience laughs)

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.250
Do you know the guy that did

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.140
the prosthetic nose for "Maestro?"

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.630
He did Mel's entire face and body

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.513
and personality for the last 97 years.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:29.640
Did you know this?

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:32.220
Mel Brooks not only produced "The Fly,"

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.860
and "The Elephant Man,"
but he took his name

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.170
and EP credit off the film,

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:40.080
so that the audience
wouldn't confuse the genre.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:40.950
Isn't that amazing?

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.410
Can you imagine anyone else in this room,

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:47.130
not taking credit for
something they actually did?

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:48.213
I cannot.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:52.980
Tonight we're also honoring
a legendary film editor,

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.399
Carol Littleton, ladies and gentlemen.

00:06:55.399 --> 00:06:58.232
(audience cheers)

00:07:02.064 --> 00:07:04.897
(audience cheers)

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:18.420
Carol, Carol Littleton's
work is incredible.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.617
She's edited "The Accidental Tourist,"

00:07:20.617 --> 00:07:23.970
"Swimming to Cambodia,"
Steven Spielberg's "ET,"

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:27.519
which before she got to it
was called "Extraterrestrial."

00:07:27.519 --> 00:07:28.352
(audience laughs)

00:07:28.352 --> 00:07:32.490
Her Lifetime Achievement
Award is in editing.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:35.845
Wouldn't it be funny if her
speech was way too long?

00:07:35.845 --> 00:07:36.678
(audience laughs)

00:07:36.678 --> 00:07:40.080
Do you know, it was
Carol Littleton's hand,

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.720
that cut a young Kevin Costner,

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.297
completely out of the
movie, "The Big Chill?"

00:07:46.789 --> 00:07:48.630
Kevin Costner was originally
in "The Big Chill."

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:51.270
He played Alex, their college
friend who's passed away,

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:54.570
and apparently Director
Lawrence Kasdan really liked

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:56.220
young Costner's performance.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.920
But it was Carol Littleton
who said, "Nah, nah,

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.173
nah, nah, nah, nah, nah.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:03.750
Not that guy."

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:06.667
(audience laughs)

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:09.007
When I saw "Oppenheimer," I was like,

00:08:09.007 --> 00:08:11.370
"Did Carol Littleton get her
hand on Rami Malek's part?

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:15.597
This has the Costner slash
written all over it."

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:20.090
I am speechless, by the
way, to be in the same room,

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:21.930
as our next honoree.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.600
The incredible Ms. Angela Bassett.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:25.658
Angela.

00:08:25.658 --> 00:08:28.478
(audience cheers)

00:08:28.478 --> 00:08:30.346
Hey, wow.

00:08:30.346 --> 00:08:33.179
(audience cheers)

00:08:39.953 --> 00:08:43.267
"Boyz n the Hood," "What's
Love Got to Do with It?"

00:08:43.267 --> 00:08:46.290
"Black Panther," she is the best.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:49.680
If I may get personal for
a second here, Angela,

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:50.970
this is extra special for me.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.670
See, growing up as a boy in the Midwest,

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.920
I went to a lot of movies,
but I never felt like

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.920
I saw myself on the big screen
until I was 12 years old.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.200
And I saw a movie called
"Waiting to Exhale,"

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.204
and I said, "That's me.

00:09:06.204 --> 00:09:08.190
(audience laughs)

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:09.900
I am Bernadine."

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.390
(audience laughs)

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:14.610
Here's what a great
actor Angela Bassett is.

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:17.910
She got a Oscar nomination
for a Marvel movie.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:21.590
That's like getting a Pulitzer
Prize for a Reddit comment.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:24.423
(audience laughs)

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:29.970
Finally, tonight, we are presenting

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.520
the Jean Hersholt Humanitarian Award

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:34.560
to the founding senior director

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:37.380
of the Sundance Institute's
Artist Programs,

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:39.071
Michelle Satter.

00:09:39.071 --> 00:09:41.988
(audience cheers)

00:09:58.725 --> 00:10:00.060
Thank you, Michelle.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:03.210
Past recipients of the
Jean Hersholt Award include

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:07.680
Frank Sinatra, Angelina
Jolie, and Tyler Perry.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:11.532
So it's really unclear
what this award means.

00:10:11.532 --> 00:10:14.449
(audience laughs)

00:10:15.586 --> 00:10:18.060
Michelle's work with the Institute,

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:21.810
has helped launch the careers
of countless film legends,

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.103
and it continues to foster

00:10:24.103 --> 00:10:26.940
the brilliant emerging voices of tomorrow.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:29.343
On behalf of everyone, thank you Michelle.

00:10:31.005 --> 00:10:33.922
(audience cheers)

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:40.430
I know we're having a lot of
fun, but it's award season.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.100
There's a lot of emotion
and expectation in the air.

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:45.240
Some of you're probably even aware

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:47.388
that voting ends in seven days.

00:10:47.388 --> 00:10:50.190
(audience laughs)

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:54.990
I was reminded recently of
something Al Pacino once said,

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:58.560
about what it's like to be an
artist at the Academy Awards,

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:01.170
and if you would indulge me,
I'd like to share it now.

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:05.793
I think it's quite apt for
this evening, and this is real.

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:10.447
So the Playboy interviewer guy says...

00:11:10.447 --> 00:11:13.920
(audience laughs)

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.350
Says, "Have you ever been to the Oscars?"

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.157
And this is what Pacino said.

00:11:18.157 --> 00:11:20.580
"I was at the Oscars once for "Serpico."

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:22.500
That was the second time I was nominated.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:24.960
I was sitting in the third or
fourth row with Diane Keaton.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:26.700
Jeff Bridges was there with his girl.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:29.490
No one expected me to
come, I was a little high.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:31.710
Somebody had done something
to my hair, blew it out

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:33.660
or something, and I looked
like I had a bird's nest

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:35.430
on my head, a real mess.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:36.930
At one point, I turned to Jeff Bridges,

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.790
and I said, "Hey, looks
like there won't be time

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:40.260
for the Best Actor awards."

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:41.340
He gave me a strange look,

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:42.870
and said, "What are you talking about?"

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:44.550
I said, "It's over, the hour is up."

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.350
He said, "It's three hours long."

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:48.180
I thought it was an hour TV show.

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:49.500
Can you believe that?

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:53.580
Also, I had to pee, bad,
so I popped a Valium.

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:55.050
Actually, I was eating Valium

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:57.240
like they were candy, chewed on them.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.040
Finally, the Best Actor Award.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.143
Can you imagine the shape I was in?

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:03.960
I couldn't have made it to the stage.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.910
I was praying, please don't let it be me.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:07.470
Please don't let it be me.

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:09.210
And then I hear, "Jack Lemmon."

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.640
I was just so happy I
didn't have to get up,

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:13.080
because I never would've made it.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:17.550
The Valium was kicking in
and I had to pee so bad."

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:19.245
On that note...

00:12:19.245 --> 00:12:21.510
(audience laughs)

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:25.170
Thank you Al, wherever you are.

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:30.170
On that note, let's begin the
14th Annual Governor's Awards.

00:12:30.756 --> 00:12:33.589
(dramatic music)

